    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Assistant-Professor-of-Urban-Planning_R-03530": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Urban Planning",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: Assistant Professor of Urban Planning Compensation: $70,000 - $75,000 Department: Urban Planning Start Date: 08/01/2027 About this opportunity: The Department of Urban Planning in the Estopinal College of Architecture and Planning is hiring a tenure track Assistant Professor of Urban Planning, starting August of 2027. Applicants with a wide variety of areas of expertise in the specialization are invited to apply. Core areas of teaching and research specialization may include but are not limited to economic development topics (workforce development, firm retention and attraction) and community development (community development corporations, organizing, neighborhood-based revitalization). We are seeking a teacher-scholar committed to the advancement of research, education, the discipline and practice of urban planning, and service to the university and community. Employer will consider sponsorship. What you'll do and what you will bring: Duties and Responsibilities:  Major responsibilities will include: 1) The production of scholarly contributions adequate for advancing a significant research agenda in the fields of community development or economic development. 2) Teaching core undergraduate and graduate lectures, seminars, and studios on either community development planning, or economic development planning as well as electives in areas of faculty specialty. 3) Service to the community, university, college and department. Minimum Qualifications: PhD in Urban Planning or an allied field. ABD will be considered provided all degree requirements are met by date of appointment. Demonstrated research and teaching focus in community-economic development as evidenced by statements of research and teaching.  Preferred Qualifications: Evidence of excellence teaching lecture, seminar and studio courses at the undergraduate and master's levels. Strong research and scholarly agenda that is commensurate with requirements for tenure.  Research or teaching interest in planning in small cities, and/ or legacy cities.  Interest in teaching in the ECAP interdisciplinary design-focused first-year undergraduate program.  AICP certification.  How to apply: To complete this application, please have ready your cover letter, curriculum vitae, teaching philosophy, and a research statement. NOTE: Do not use the Autofill feature for completion of application. Questions about this position should be directed to Dr. John H. West, chair of the department, at planning@bsu.edu This vacancy will be posted until the position is filled. Top consideration will be given to candidates whose applications are received by Dec. 4, 2026. What we offer: At Ball State University, we understand the importance of balancing professional responsibilities with personal well-being. That's why we offer 12 paid sick days and 10 holidays a year. Plus, our paid parental leave (6 weeks) is there for life's most significant moments. We offer affordable health and dental insurance, robust long-term disability and life insurance plans, as well a range of retirement benefits to provide financial security to you and your family. These benefits ensure that you can focus on your work and personal growth without worrying about the unexpected. When you work for us, you are family. And to show our support of your family, we will cover 90 percent of your child's (or children's) undergraduate college tuition at Ball State. This offer is good from the very first day you start. It's just one way we do our part to uplift you. Let's fly together toward a secure and prosperous tomorrow. Curious about our excellent benefits package? We have developed a calculator that shows you the value of our core benefits. Select the Total Compensation Calculator - Faculty and download our calculator spreadsheet. Relocation Program: MakeMyMove   Looking for a place where your career, community, and quality of life can grow together? Muncie and Delaware County offer the advantages of a university community with the accessibility and affordability of a mid-sized city—short commutes, local arts and culture, parks and trails, educational opportunities, healthcare, athletics, and a strong sense of connection. Through the City of Muncie’s MakeMyMove program, eligible new residents may access relocation incentives, university-related benefits, co-working options, and community-building opportunities designed to help them get settled and connected. Current benefits for eligible participants may include: Up to $5,000 cash for relocation Co-working space courtesy of The Innovation Connector Access to the Ball State University Library One-semester Rec Fit pass at Ball State University Discounted Ball State Athletics tickets Help desk for connecting to local resources Opportunity for board participation Six-month YMCA membership Relocation programs on MakeMyMove have specific eligibility requirements, and benefits may vary by program. Applicants should review the current MakeMyMove page to confirm eligibility, available benefits, and program status. Ball State’s support for relocation resources and community partnerships aligns with Destination 2040: Our Flight Path , the University’s strategic plan. The plan calls our University to work with community partners to promote talent attraction and retention, foster economic growth in East Central Indiana, and strengthen the economic and social vitality of the communities we serve. Explore the program and check your eligibility through MakeMyMove ."
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Region-4-Coalition-Manager_R-03541": {
      "title": "Region 4 Coalition Manager",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: Region 4 Coalition Manager Compensation: $60,000-$75,000 Department: College of Health Deans Office About this opportunity: The College of Health welcomes applications for the position of Region 4 Coalition Manager to begin soon. This position is a vital component of the Growing Rural Opportunities for Well-being (GROW) Regional Grant Program that is part of Indiana’s participation in the nationwide Rural Health Transformation Program funded by the federal One Big Beautiful Bill. The successful applicant will coordinate efforts with 24 grant-funded community agency stakeholders across 5 areas of initiatives in 10 rural counties in the 12-county region in East Central Indiana. More information about GROW Regional Grant Program is available at https://www.in.gov/grow-rural-health/. We seek applicants with experience in community organizing, community development, or public affairs to manage, coordinate and support the Region 4 Coalition and participating agencies. Candidates for searches must have current authorization to be employed in the U.S. without employer sponsorship. Please note: This position is funded through a grant and is approved for the first year of the grant only; during which time, employment is at-will. The position may be extended additional years should funding continue and program requirements and outcomes are achieved. If the grant is not renewed, the position will end at the conclusion of the one-year funding period. Continued employment opportunities may be available through other grants, contracts, partnerships, or organizational funding, but would depend on funding availability, program needs, and organizational approval at that time. Steven’s Amendment: This Rural Health Transformation Program is supported by the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) of the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) as part of a financial assistance award totaling $206,927,896.80 with 100 percent funded by CMS/HHS. The contents are those of the author(s) and do not necessarily represent the official views of, nor an endorsement, by CMS/HHS, or the U.S. Government. What you'll do and what you'll bring: Position Function: Reporting to the Dean of the College of Health, the Regional Coalition Manager will manage, coordinate and drive collaboration among 24 funded agencies for Region 4 (12 counties in East Central Indiana) of the Growing Rural Opportunities for Well-being (GROW) Regional Grant Program with the Indiana Rural Health Transformation Program (RHTP). Initiatives include 1) expanding community-based access to care; 2) implementing a regionwide maternal health access model; 3) transforming behavioral health and crisis response; 4) strengthening emergency medical response and rural clinical readiness; and, 5) building a long-term rural healthcare workforce pipeline. The Regional Coalition Manager will work directly with the Region 4 Coalition leadership and members to establish governance and oversight, provide central coordination for the 24 funded organizations, and work with the Regional Grant Coordinator to monitor outcomes. The Regional Coalition Manager must be able to work independently and collaboratively. Although the position base will be located at Ball State University in Muncie, the work with the Region 4 Coalition and funded partners will involve frequent travel in a 12-county region and may require virtual arrangements. More information about GROW and RHTP, including a map of the counties, is available at https://www.in.gov/grow-rural-health/regional-grants/#Region_4 . Duties & Responsibilities: In collaboration with the Coalition and its leadership, establish and oversee governance, including establishing formal bylaws, policies, election procedures, and committees. Serve as anchor and liaison to manage communication as well as convene and lead monthly and as needed meetings with across and between 5 workgroups for the 12-county region and 24 funded organizations within the 10 rural counties. In concert with state leadership, develop workshops to assist the Coalition with evaluation and state reporting needs.  In collaboration with the Coalition, develop and implement a unified regional framework with the Coalition leadership, aligning activities to reduce silos and duplication. Manage ongoing communication between sub-grantees, support coalition membership governance, and assist with website development. Collaborate with Coalition leadership for organization-level strategic planning and sustainability efforts. Meet regularly with the Indiana Department of Health and the Regional Grant Coordinator to plan communication with the 24 agency grantees to reinforce state expectations. Perform other related duties as assigned. Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree in a health, social service, political science or related discipline. Three years of experience in community advocacy, government, and/or community development. (Relevant experiences may include a combination of prior work in stakeholder engagement, grassroots advocacy, coalition building, community engagement, project management.) Possess and maintain a valid driver's license and be insurable under the university's auto fleet policy. Must be willing and able to travel frequently in a 12-county region. Preferred Qualifications: Master’s degree in public administration, public health, health administration, social work or related disciplines. Five to 10 years in increasingly responsible management experience. Experience or familiarity with legislative affairs and processes as well as grant writing are desirable. How to apply: Questions regarding this opportunity may be directed to: Scott Rutledge, Dean of the College of Health Scott.Rutledge@bsu.edu Required in this application: Cover letter addressing qualifications for the position. Resume or curriculum vitae. Contact information for three professional references. Applications must be received before 12:01 AM on November 14, 2026. This position is funded through the Growing Rural Opportunities for Well-being (GROW) Regional Grant Program under the Indiana Rural Health Transformation Program (RHTP) . Employment is contingent upon continued grant funding. What we offer: At Ball State University, we understand the importance of balancing professional responsibilities with personal well-being. That's why we offer paid 24 vacation days, 12 sick days and 10 holidays a year. Plus, our paid parental leave (6 weeks) is there for life's most significant moments. We offer affordable health and dental insurance, robust long-term disability and life insurance plans, as well a range of retirement benefits to provide financial security to you and your family. These benefits ensure that you can focus on your work and personal growth without worrying about the unexpected. When you work for us, you are family. And to show our support of your family, we will cover 90 percent of your child's (or children's) undergraduate college tuition at Ball State. We do the same for you.  In fact, we'll cover 100 percent of the tuition costs for you to earn your undergraduate degree and 80 percent of your graduate tuition.  This offer is good from the very first day you start. It's just one way we do our part to uplift you. Let's fly together toward a secure and prosperous tomorrow. Curious about our excellent benefits package? We have developed a calculator that shows you the value of our core benefits. Select the Total Compensation Calculator - Professional and download the spreadsheet. If you are applying for a 10-month position or one with Burris or Indiana Academy, please select the corresponding calculator. Relocation Program: MakeMyMove   Looking for a place where your career, community, and quality of life can grow together? Muncie and Delaware County offer the advantages of a university community with the accessibility and affordability of a mid-sized city—short commutes, local arts and culture, parks and trails, educational opportunities, healthcare, athletics, and a strong sense of connection. Through the City of Muncie’s MakeMyMove program, eligible new residents may access relocation incentives, university-related benefits, co-working options, and community-building opportunities designed to help them get settled and connected. Current benefits for eligible participants may include: Up to $5,000 cash for relocation Co-working space courtesy of The Innovation Connector Access to the Ball State University Library One-semester Rec Fit pass at Ball State University Discounted Ball State Athletics tickets Help desk for connecting to local resources Opportunity for board participation Six-month YMCA membership Relocation programs on MakeMyMove have specific eligibility requirements, and benefits may vary by program. Applicants should review the current MakeMyMove page to confirm eligibility, available benefits, and program status. Ball State’s support for relocation resources and community partnerships aligns with Destination 2040: Our Flight Path , the University’s strategic plan. The plan calls our University to work with community partners to promote talent attraction and retention, foster economic growth in East Central Indiana, and strengthen the economic and social vitality of the communities we serve. Explore the program and check your eligibility through MakeMyMove ."
    },
    "https://bsu.wd12.myworkdayjobs.com/External/job/Ball-State-University/Assistant-Professor-of-Architecture_R-03514": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Architecture",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-10-29\nFull time\n\nPosition Title: Assistant Professor of Architecture Compensation: $70,000 - $75,000 Department: Architecture Start Date: 08/16/2027 About this opportunity: Primary responsibilities include undergraduate and graduate level architecture, with an emphasis on Design Studio and Communications. The expectations are that the candidate is to be fluent in digital medias and innovative design and representational strategies. The candidate is expected to be an active participant in the governance and service of the department. Ideal candidate demonstrates evidence of excellence in teaching within an architectural curriculum; evidence of productive scholarship (research/creative work) with an active research, grant solicitation, and publication agenda; design studio teaching; excellence and experience in teaching a primary AND secondary topical area. Position is on-campus/in-person Position to start in Fall 2027 Academic Semester. Employer will consider sponsorship. What you'll do and what you will bring: Position Function: Primary responsibilities include undergraduate and graduate level architecture, with an emphasis on Design Studio and Communications. The expectations are that the candidate is to be fluent in digital medias and innovative design and representational strategies. The candidate is expected to be an active participant in the governance and service of the department. The Estopinal College of Architecture and Planning (CAP) at Ball State University has a rich history in community engaged, professionally grounded, innovative, and impactful design education. The Department of Architecture offers undergraduate and graduate architecture programs that emphasize the education of architecture and historic preservation students through design studio, engagement with social and environmental justice issues, immersive and experiential learning, design computation, high performance building design, building systems and assemblies, digital fabrication, and critical practice in the pursuit of preparing the next generation of leadership in architecture. Duties & Responsibilities: Major responsibilities include: A base load of 24 hours per academic year consisting of teaching undergraduate and graduate level students (design studio and lecture/seminar courses in innovative design methodologies and critical contemporary communication), and productive research/scholarship. The candidate will be expected to perform other related duties and service as assigned by Department Chairperson. Minimum Qualifications: 1. Master of Architecture by time of appointment OR; 2. Bachelor of Architecture with post-professional master’s degree in architecture or allied discipline from an accredited college or university by time of appointment. Preferred Qualifications: Evidence of excellence in teaching within an architectural curriculum; evidence of productive scholarship (research/creative work) with an active research, grant solicitation, and publication agenda; design studio teaching; excellence and experience in teaching a primary AND secondary topical area. How to apply: For inquiries, please contact: Sean Burns - sfburns@bsu.edu To ensure full consideration, applications must be received by JANUARY 1, 2027 (Applications will continue to be accepted after the full consideration date of January 1, 2027 until a sufficient applicant pool has been achieved or the position is filled. The closing date is subject to change without notice.) Please follow upload instructions to attach the required supporting documents listed below. Note: Do not use the Auto fill feature for resume: • Cover Letter • Resume/Curriculum Vitae • Portfolio What we offer: At Ball State University, we understand the importance of balancing professional responsibilities with personal well-being. That's why we offer 12 paid sick days and 10 holidays a year. Plus, our paid parental leave (6 weeks) is there for life's most significant moments. We offer affordable health and dental insurance, robust long-term disability and life insurance plans, as well a range of retirement benefits to provide financial security to you and your family. These benefits ensure that you can focus on your work and personal growth without worrying about the unexpected. When you work for us, you are family. And to show our support of your family, we will cover 90 percent of your child's (or children's) undergraduate college tuition at Ball State. This offer is good from the very first day you start. It's just one way we do our part to uplift you. Let's fly together toward a secure and prosperous tomorrow. Curious about our excellent benefits package? We have developed a calculator that shows you the value of our core benefits. Select the Total Compensation Calculator - Faculty and download our calculator spreadsheet. Relocation Program: MakeMyMove   Looking for a place where your career, community, and quality of life can grow together? Muncie and Delaware County offer the advantages of a university community with the accessibility and affordability of a mid-sized city—short commutes, local arts and culture, parks and trails, educational opportunities, healthcare, athletics, and a strong sense of connection. Through the City of Muncie’s MakeMyMove program, eligible new residents may access relocation incentives, university-related benefits, co-working options, and community-building opportunities designed to help them get settled and connected. Current benefits for eligible participants may include: Up to $5,000 cash for relocation Co-working space courtesy of The Innovation Connector Access to the Ball State University Library One-semester Rec Fit pass at Ball State University Discounted Ball State Athletics tickets Help desk for connecting to local resources Opportunity for board participation Six-month YMCA membership Relocation programs on MakeMyMove have specific eligibility requirements, and benefits may vary by program. Applicants should review the current MakeMyMove page to confirm eligibility, available benefits, and program status. Ball State’s support for relocation resources and community partnerships aligns with Destination 2040: Our Flight Path , the University’s strategic plan. The plan calls our University to work with community partners to promote talent attraction and retention, foster economic growth in East Central Indiana, and strengthen the economic and social vitality of the communities we serve. Explore the program and check your eligibility through MakeMyMove ."
